#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167580} :activity/id #uuid "494e7d10-63cc-5ac1-aeb6-77e53987c48f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048333 :topic/id #uuid "95fa9a7c-5a38-5e0a-9686-d1451bc45075" :topic/math-academy-id 3212} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167580 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168064 :tutorial/content "We've already seen how to write the coordinates of a vector $\\mathbf v\\in \\mathbb R^n$ relative some general basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\mathbb R^n.$ In this lesson, we will generalize this concept to abstract vector spaces.\n\nAs an example, consider the matrices $A_1$ and $A_2,$ given by\n\n![](images/28/2828c037a750f078b4d98b93248e800ec05d4899d7eb390dd135326314c44e70.svg)\n\nThe set $\\mathcal{B}=\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ is a basis of the vector space $V = \\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}.$ Now, suppose that the coordinates of the matrix $C$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are \n\n![](images/2d/2db3ab1e4452eff2f19151cc87a805452433c7f305a80e5480c25241cba42c38.svg)\n\nLet's find the matrix $C$ using this information. \n\nAs usual, any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ A_1, A_2 \\big\\}$ can be written as\n\n$$\nx_1 A_1  + x_2 A_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1,x_2$ are the coordinates of the vector relative to the basis. \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n![](images/64/6489e130a9237881664e1649cc5408cdff87cfb12dcf44c0342b9961cdf7bc81.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 12 \\\\ 8 & -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 10 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6638787f-3ef3-5623-bcec-de4b89ebfd02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4962 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e61da532-1350-59ec-be76-0a0464423ad6" :step/math-academy-id 653397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167581}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166751 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166589 :question/id #uuid "4dac81da-f15c-55ff-9630-7349a3e03e57" :question/math-academy-id "e-8273"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf357ee2-b8b4-538c-b269-f7546cf5374f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048552}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483390}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Coordinates Relative to a Basis"} :step/id #uuid "9a4ae83d-c5c3-5b9f-bd08-97b2e0bc398a" :step/math-academy-id 653398 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166590 :question/id #uuid "aac4b559-b26b-57b0-8701-b3235969cd2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8274"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a6b6e1-fb99-5ebe-9f3f-000878f79f0d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483409}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector With Respect to a Given Basis in a Matrix Vector Space"} :step/id #uuid "8e896c1e-d15c-5ab8-ab0f-39877293d9e4" :step/math-academy-id 653401 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166753 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166591 :question/id #uuid "f0fc412f-8276-5876-a509-4f2173396e72" :question/math-academy-id "e-8275"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d8f8be0-0fe9-5734-87d3-5c432b7f298f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483426}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector With Respect to a Given Basis in a Polynomial Vector Space"} :step/id #uuid "6b14a3b6-adad-5d10-bce3-5e05e7cf5144" :step/math-academy-id 653404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168065 :tutorial/content "Suppose we fix a basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ of a finite-dimensional vector space $V$ with scalars from $\\mathbb{R}$ (note that \"finite-dimensional\" means that the basis contains a finite number of elements). Then, we can represent any element $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ using the coordinates of $\\mathbf{v}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c9/c9297a30f87baf08295a752cb23f2d314139508f875b4ffa2fffc8763656bd54.svg)\n\nThis defines a bijective mapping $\\phi: V\\to \\mathbb{R}^n.$ Moreover, this particular mapping has some nice properties:\n\n- $\\phi(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}) = \\phi(\\mathbf{u}) + \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in V,$ and\n\n- $\\phi(\\alpha \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\phi(\\mathbf{v})$ for any $\\mathbf{v} \\in V$ and any $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nBecause of this, we say that $V$ is **isomorphic** to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Intuitively, this means that $V$ and $\\mathbb R^n$ have identical structures.\n\nSo, any finite-dimensional vector space is isomorphic to $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for some particular $n.$ This means that instead of studying all possible vector spaces, we can simply study only one family of vector spaces, namely $\\mathbb{R}^n$ for $n\\geq 1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e501b24f-7ea0-5110-a4c5-e8739ea9ac11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4964 :tutorial/title "Isomorphisms of Vector Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "f13dd7dd-f256-5871-9c5b-37fcd35b85a3" :step/math-academy-id 653407}] :activity/title "The Coordinates of a Vector Relative to a Basis in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166996}]}
